  <translations>
    <!-- English is the source language and needs no entry here. Every other
         locale listed in <outputs> above that isn't listed here falls back
         to the English source text at build time, because of
         fallback_to_english="true" below. -->
    <file path="translations/nbrowser_first_run_strings_ru.xtb" lang="ru" />
  </translations>

  <release seq="1">
    <messages fallback_to_english="true">
      <message name="IDS_NBROWSER_FIRST_RUN_TITLE" desc="Browser tab title for the NBrowser first-run/welcome page.">
        Welcome to NBrowser
      </message>
      <message name="IDS_NBROWSER_FIRST_RUN_HERO_HEADING" desc="Main heading in the hero section of the first-run page.">
        Welcome to NBrowser
      </message>
      <message name="IDS_NBROWSER_FIRST_RUN_HERO_SUBTEXT" desc="Subheading/thank-you paragraph under the hero heading.">
        Thanks for giving it a try. NBrowser starts from the same engine as Chrome, but with the parts that quietly report on you removed — not hidden, not just switched off, actually removed. Here's what that means for you.
      </message>
      <message name="IDS_NBROWSER_FIRST_RUN_PHILOSOPHY_HEADING" desc="Heading for the philosophy/why-we-do-this section.">
        Our philosophy, in plain terms
      </message>
      <message name="IDS_NBROWSER_FIRST_RUN_PHILOSOPHY_INTRO" desc="Intro paragraph before the three philosophy cards.">
        We know "privacy-focused browser" is a claim everyone makes. So instead of asking you to trust us, we'd rather explain exactly what we do — in language anyone can follow, not just developers.
      </message>
      <message name="IDS_NBROWSER_FIRST_RUN_PHILOSOPHY_CUT_TITLE" desc="Title of the 'cut not disable' philosophy card.">
        We cut it out, we don't just turn it off
      </message>
      <message name="IDS_NBROWSER_FIRST_RUN_PHILOSOPHY_CUT_BODY" desc="Body text of the 'cut not disable' philosophy card.">
        Most browsers let you switch off usage tracking in a settings menu. The trouble is, a setting can be silently switched back on by an update, a misclick, or a change you never notice. NBrowser takes a different approach: the code that would collect your usage data, send crash reports to a company, or tag your device with a unique identifier isn't disabled — it's removed from the program entirely. Because NBrowser is open source, this isn't something you have to take our word for: anyone can look at the code and see for themselves that it's gone.
      </message>
      <message name="IDS_NBROWSER_FIRST_RUN_PHILOSOPHY_TERRITORIES_TITLE" desc="Title of the 'two territories' philosophy card.">
        Two territories: the browser, and the internet
      </message>
      <message name="IDS_NBROWSER_FIRST_RUN_PHILOSOPHY_TERRITORIES_BODY" desc="Body text of the 'two territories' philosophy card.">
        It helps to think of your privacy in two parts. There's "browser territory" — what NBrowser itself does, which is: nothing, when you simply open it. No silent check-ins, no usage reports, no phoning home. Then there's "internet territory" — what the websites you visit choose to do, like setting cookies or trying to fingerprint your device. NBrowser can't make that choice for you, and we don't think it should try to. Instead, our goal is to keep giving you real tools — like choosing your own secure DNS provider or controlling exactly how cookies behave — so you can decide, deliberately, how much you want to share as you browse.
      </message>
      <message name="IDS_NBROWSER_FIRST_RUN_PHILOSOPHY_PRAGMATIC_TITLE" desc="Title of the 'practical not extreme' philosophy card.">
        Practical, not extreme
      </message>
      <message name="IDS_NBROWSER_FIRST_RUN_PHILOSOPHY_PRAGMATIC_BODY" desc="Body text of the 'practical not extreme' philosophy card.">
        We're not chasing zero network requests at any cost — that would make for a worse, less useful browser. NBrowser still keeps your clock in sync, still updates itself, and still fetches the odd small essential piece, like fresh root certificates or the Widevine component needed to play some video. The difference is where those requests go and what they carry: browser updates come from our own GitHub releases instead of Google's update servers, and none of it is used to build a profile of you.
      </message>
      <message name="IDS_NBROWSER_FIRST_RUN_FEATURES_HEADING" desc="Heading for the concrete features/reasons-to-stay section.">
        What you're actually getting
      </message>
      <message name="IDS_NBROWSER_FIRST_RUN_FEATURE_CUT_TITLE" desc="Title of the telemetry-removed feature card.">
        Telemetry, physically removed
      </message>
      <message name="IDS_NBROWSER_FIRST_RUN_FEATURE_CUT_BODY" desc="Body of the telemetry-removed feature card.">
        No usage analytics, no crash reporting to a third party, no device or install identifiers, and no background fetching of Google's experiment configuration.
      </message>
      <message name="IDS_NBROWSER_FIRST_RUN_FEATURE_UPDATES_TITLE" desc="Title of the updates-without-tracking feature card.">
        Updates without the tracking
      </message>
      <message name="IDS_NBROWSER_FIRST_RUN_FEATURE_UPDATES_BODY" desc="Body of the updates-without-tracking feature card.">
        NBrowser updates itself using our own GitHub releases instead of Google's Omaha update service, so the update process itself isn't a source of usage telemetry.
      </message>
      <message name="IDS_NBROWSER_FIRST_RUN_FEATURE_OPEN_TITLE" desc="Title of the open-source/verifiable feature card.">
        Nothing to take on faith
      </message>
      <message name="IDS_NBROWSER_FIRST_RUN_FEATURE_OPEN_BODY" desc="Body of the open-source/verifiable feature card.">
        NBrowser is open source. Every claim on this page is something you — or anyone — can go verify directly in the code, rather than a promise you have to trust.
      </message>
      <message name="IDS_NBROWSER_FIRST_RUN_FEATURE_CONTROLS_TITLE" desc="Title of the privacy-controls-preserved feature card.">
        Privacy tools that stay in your hands
      </message>
      <message name="IDS_NBROWSER_FIRST_RUN_FEATURE_CONTROLS_BODY" desc="Body of the privacy-controls-preserved feature card.">
        The secure DNS settings and cookie controls you'd expect from a modern browser are still here, fully intact, so you can shape your own privacy setup instead of having it decided for you.
      </message>
      <message name="IDS_NBROWSER_FIRST_RUN_FEATURE_ROADMAP_BADGE" desc="Small badge label on the roadmap feature card, meaning 'not shipped yet'.">
        Coming soon
      </message>
      <message name="IDS_NBROWSER_FIRST_RUN_FEATURE_ROADMAP_TITLE" desc="Title of the roadmap/not-yet-shipped feature card.">
        What we're building next
      </message>
      <message name="IDS_NBROWSER_FIRST_RUN_FEATURE_ROADMAP_BODY" desc="Body of the roadmap/not-yet-shipped feature card. Must not imply these features exist today.">
        We're working toward more tools for assembling your own privacy posture, such as built-in VPN support and fingerprint protection. These aren't available yet — we'd rather tell you honestly what's coming than claim it's already here.
      </message>
      <message name="IDS_NBROWSER_FIRST_RUN_EXTENSIONS_HEADING" desc="Heading for the brief extensions section.">
        Extensions, the easy way
      </message>
      <message name="IDS_NBROWSER_FIRST_RUN_EXTENSIONS_BODY" desc="Body text of the brief extensions section, referencing the pre-installed web-store helper extension. Deliberately avoids the literal word 'Chromium' (see risk notes in the implementation plan about a branding sweep script).">
        NBrowser comes with a web-store helper extension already installed (built by NeverDecaf), so you can browse and install extensions from the Chrome Web Store just like you would in any other browser built on the same engine — no extra setup required.
      </message>
      <message name="IDS_NBROWSER_FIRST_RUN_FOOTER_REVISIT" desc="Footer line telling the user how to find this page again. Must contain the literal URL chrome://nbrowser-first-run.">
        You can always find this page again at chrome://nbrowser-first-run.
      </message>
      <message name="IDS_NBROWSER_FIRST_RUN_FOOTER_DONATE" desc="Understated, low-visual-weight donate/support link text at the very bottom of the page.">
        If NBrowser's been useful to you, you can support its development here.
      </message>
    </messages>
  </release>
</grit>
